From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mempage4k <mempage4k@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 01:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wgpu-hal: Metal command buffers made when first needed

wgpu-core begins encoding for each render or compute pass and again for
the barriers before it, which are no-ops on Metal: begin_encoding made a
Metal command buffer each time, so about half of them stayed empty. Each
still cost what Metal charges per command buffer: made (the IOGPU storage
and frame-pacing metrics), handed to the kernel when committed, then
reset and released after completion. In a game on an iPhone 16 Pro,
Metal's own threads took 30 % of the process's CPU time, 13 % in the
kernel call that submits command buffers.

Now begin_encoding only notes the label, and the command buffer is made
when a command first needs it (a blit, render, compute or acceleration
structure encoder, a debug group, or raw_command_buffer). An encoding
with nothing recorded ends as a CommandBuffer without one, which submit
skips. Same game: Metal's threads 23 %, the submit call 8 %.

The budget check (MAX_COMMAND_BUFFERS) stays in begin_encoding; the
count of made but unsubmitted command buffers grows when one is made.
---
